            <ArticleTitle>Iliac-to-isolated hepatic artery bypass in the treatment of intestinal angina: two cases.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1115-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Two cases in which a severe form of intestinal angina was cured by revascularization of an isolated hepatic artery were studied. The collateral pathways to the hepatic artery may serve, in a retrograde fashion, to assure an acceptable blood supply to the splanchnic organs, even when the disease of the 3 main vessels is so extensive that the commonly used techniques of revascularization cannot be applied.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
